  {
   "id": "stress",
   "level": "A1",
   "el": "Ο τόνος",
   "title": "Stress and the accent mark",
   "group": "sounds",
   "body": [
    {
     "list": [
      "Every word of two or more syllables has **one** accent (τόνος) on the stressed vowel: **καφές, θάλασσα, μήλο**.",
      "One-syllable words have no accent: **και, το, μου, τι** ... except the pairs that need one to be told apart: **ή** \"or\" (η \"the\"), **πού** \"where?\" (που \"that\"), **πώς** \"how?\" (πως \"that\").",
      "Stress can fall only on one of the **last three** syllables.",
      "On a pair, the accent goes on the **second** vowel: **ούτε, είναι, παίζω**. On capitals at the start of a word it stands before the letter: **Όλγα, Άρης**. All-capital words take no accent: **ΑΘΗΝΑ**."
     ]
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Changing stress changes the word",
       ""
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "**πότε** \"when?\"",
        "**ποτέ** \"never\""
       ],
       [
        "**γέρος** \"old man\"",
        "**γερός** \"strong\""
       ],
       [
        "**νόμος** \"law\"",
        "**νομός** \"prefecture\""
       ],
       [
        "**χώρος** \"space\"",
        "**χορός** \"dance\""
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "p": "**The extra accent.** When a weak pronoun follows a word stressed on its third-from-last syllable, the word gets a second accent on its last syllable: **το αυτοκίνητό μου, διάβασέ το, βλέποντάς τον**. With two pronouns after a word stressed on its second-from-last syllable: **δώσε μού το**."
    },
    {
     "tip": "**Ο φίλος μου είπε** \"my friend said\" but **Ο φίλος μού είπε** \"the friend told me\": here an accent on the weak pronoun shows it belongs to the verb."
    },
    {
